WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.842 --> 00:00:34.800
ZISL SLEPOVITCH: Hello.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.110
My name is Zisl
Slepovitch, and I'm

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.410
musician in residence at Yale
University's Fortunoff Video

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.080
Archive for Holocaust
Testimonies.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.590
This video series is designed
as a companion to our album,

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.560
Where Is Our Homeland?
which is the first volume

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.610
in the archive's ongoing effort
to present songs and poems

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.680
performed by survivors
in testimonies

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.940
given to the Fortunoff Archive
over the last 40 years.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.620
You can learn more
about the project

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.510
on the archive's website,
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.890
Where Is Our Homeland--

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.080
Songs From
Testimonies Volume One

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.550
was recorded in 2019 by the
Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble,

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.700
featuring renowned
singer Sashe Lurje;

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.460
Craig Judelman on the
violin; Joshua Camp

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.220
on the accordion,
keyboards, and guitar;

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.750
Dmitry Ishenko on the bass;
and myself on woodwinds.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.910
The songs in this
collection differ vastly

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.300
in terms of style, social
and historical background,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.710
language, and many
other aspects.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.240
But collectively, they create
a multidimensional picture

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.404
of Jewish life before
and during World War II.

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:48.950
Liubov K. was born in 1921
in Zvenigorodka, Ukraine.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.860
The four pieces-- two
songs and two poems--

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:57.800
she performed during her 1994
testimony about her experiences

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.490
in the labor camps
surrounding her hometown are

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:03.980
unique historical
documents telling

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:08.960
the story of the camp prisoners'
everyday struggle for survival.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.700
The camps themselves, the nearby
villages where the inmates used

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.660
to escape to get food,
and Stepan, camp guard

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.350
are named in the two
of the four texts.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:25.700
"In dem kleimen Dorf,
in Smiltschenzi"--

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.610
"In the small village
of Smiltschenzi"--

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.840
is a fascinating example of
the native Yiddish speakers

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.260
to compose a song in
German under the harshest

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.710
of conditions.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.150
The result is a mixed
almost, macaronic text,

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.500
largely in German with a
few Yiddishisms mixed in.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:49.190
The song conveys the
suffering of Jewish mothers

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.980
who were separated
from their children

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.320
and longed for their homes.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.860
The style and form
of the song are

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:01.070
reminiscent of the
Weimar Republic period

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:05.270
cabaret and theater songs,
very much in the spirit

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.280
of Kurt Weill.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.370
An indirect clue
that the song may not

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.980
have been originally composed
by the labor camp inmates in--

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.400
is the widespread use of
popular songs with new texts

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.020
during World War II.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.020
