00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.100
SARAH GARIBOVA: Smiltschenzi
was the second camp

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.440
Liubov was taken
to during her time

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:20.040
as a forced laborer on the
Transit Highway IV Project

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.540
in German-occupied Ukraine.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.410
She was there from the
early summer of 1942

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.930
until December of that year.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:30.930
As in Nemorozh, the
previous camp, Liubov

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.670
and her fellow prisoners
lived in a stable

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:36.870
and slept on straw on the floor.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.060
In these camps,
the constant threat

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.730
of punitive executions
and mass shootings

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:43.950
hung over the prisoners.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.110
In their early
days in the camps,

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.330
the prisoners did
not fully understand

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:52.230
the brutality of the German
and Ukrainian camp guards.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:55.710
Little did they know that the
German occupiers had designated

00:07:55.710 --> 00:08:00.930
anyone incapable of work as,
quote, "a superfluous mouth"

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.420
doomed to execution.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:08.040
As Liubov recalls, quote, "We
had just arrived here to work,

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.100
and we didn't know yet whether
we had the privilege of getting

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:12.510
sick or not.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.720
Four people fell
ill, three women--

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.790
two women, one girl--

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.320
and a boy.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:20.950
So they stayed in the barn.

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:22.800
They didn't go to work.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.440
That evening when we
returned from work,

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.960
the gendarmerie
turned up unexpectedly

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.740
and had us line up
outside along the stable.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.449
They went inside.

00:08:33.449 --> 00:08:36.390
The sick people were
lying in their beds.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:40.770
They pulled them up and shot
them behind the other stable."

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.740
The girl who was sick
managed to run away,

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.540
but when she reached the
fence stile to escape,

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:50.160
she was met by Silich,
a Ukrainian guard.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:54.030
As Liubov recalls, quote,
"He grabbed her by the hair

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:55.530
and brought her back.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:58.530
They shot her," end quote.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:02.940
What happened next is even
more heart-wrenching, though.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.180
Like many of the young
folks in this labor camp,

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.960
the young girl who had been
shot had relatives still alive

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.000
in the Zvenigorodka ghetto.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.640
And at the time,
relatives from the ghetto

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:16.500
would periodically
visit the camp

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.990
to bring their loved ones food.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.890
This girl's mother was among
those regular visitors.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:26.400
After the girl was shot,
Liubov and her fellow prisoners

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.080
couldn't bring
themselves to tell

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:29.800
the mother what had happened.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.120
So they told her to ask
one of the guards instead.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.990
He simply said, I can't
tell you anything.

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:38.940
The mother persisted.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:42.060
The prisoners finally told her
that the girl had fallen ill

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:45.210
and that it was forbidden
to enter the camp.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.700
Rather than
deterring the mother,

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.030
this prompted her to return
to the camp for the next three

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:54.000
days to bring food
for her daughter.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.580
And this lasted until
the politsai told her

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.800
what had actually happened.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.240
The mother lost her mind
and then was also shot.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.830
Such stories played out over
and over again in the camps,

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:11.130
inspiring the words
of the song, quote,

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:15.630
"A child without a mother,
a mother without a child.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.980
The tears we've wept
could be rivers.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:21.180
The blood we've spilled
could be an ocean."

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:25.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

